            <ArticleTitle>Does 'normal' vision improve with spinal manipulation?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>415-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To discuss a patient whose spinal abnormalities may have caused subliminal visual field loss.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CLINICAL FEATURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 22-yr-old man suffered from a painful neck. His vision was in the normal range, as measured by computerized static perimetry.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION AND OUTCOME" NlmCategory="RESULTS">To define a guideline for a proposed investigation into visual field changes with spinal adjustment, his visual fields were tested before and after a normal office spinal manipulation. After this procedure, there was a measurable rise in the visual sensitivity of both eyes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The use of computerized static perimetry changes to measure the cerebral effects of spinal manipulation is recommended for future chiropractic research.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
